What this solution actually does: SWF_OUT_PK_DB is the hand-off stage of the outbound SWIFT chain. It is a timer-driven Windows Service (the host falls back to 10 seconds, ServiceHost.cs:33 (../../SWIFT/Outgoing/SWF_OUT_PK_DB/SWF_OUT_PK_DB/ServiceHost.cs#L33); the configured interval lives in the encrypted service configuration and is not visible here). Each tick opens a SimpleInjector scope and OperacionServicio.EjecutaProcedimientosAsync (../../SWIFT/Outgoing/SWF_OUT_PK_DB/Aplicacion/OperacionServicio.cs#L48) (1) marks the service as running, (2) loads three system parameters (HORA_OP_INICIO, HORA_OP_FIN, SWF_OUT_GET_MT_BATCH), the database business date, and the business-day flag, (3) skips the tick with a CRITICAL alert when the local time is outside the operating window, (4) on a business day calls the repository, and (5) marks the service as finished in a finally. The repository (OperacionesOUTPK.cs (../../SWIFT/Outgoing/SWF_OUT_PK_DB/Infraestructura/Repositorio/OperacionesOUTPK.cs)) selects every SWIFT_OUT work-plan master row in status AUT (authorized) that has a work row, and for each one executes usp_swf_inserta_pk_db, which (a) inserts a control row INI into tb_srv_swf_swift_out_ctl, (b) moves the master row from AUT to MQ, and (c) closes the work-plan detail rows. The control table is what the two MQ senders poll (SWF_OUT_MQ for everything except MT-998, SWF_OUT_MQ_GCMS for MT-998; both select cve_estatus = 'INI'), so this service is the only gate between "authorized" and "sent to the SWIFT gateway". The business meaning of the status codes, the downstream senders' idempotency, and the DDL of the three tables are inferred from the SQL project and are not documented in this repository.
