PKDB-FR-3 (High) - The insert procedure is not atomic or idempotent, so a partial failure re-inserts the control row and can make a SWIFT message go out twice
• File:line: 004 SP usp_SWF_INSERTA_PK_DB.sql:27-38 (../../StoredProcedures/MWD_DB/DBMDW/SWIFT/Outgoing/SWF_OUT_PK_DB/IT24/Procedures/004%20SP%20usp_SWF_INSERTA_PK_DB.sql#L27) (called from OperacionesOUTPK.cs:102-108 (../../SWIFT/Outgoing/SWF_OUT_PK_DB/Infraestructura/Repositorio/OperacionesOUTPK.cs#L102))
• Current code:
``sql INSERT INTO tb_srv_swf_swift_out_ctl (folio, tipo_operacion, cve_estatus, fec_adicion) VALUES (@icn, @tipo_operacion, 'INI', GetDate()); UPDATE tb_srv_swf_plan_trabajo_maestro SET cve_estatus='MQ', ... WHERE ... AND cve_estatus='AUT'; UPDATE tb_srv_swf_plan_trabajo_detalle SET flg_terminado=1, ... WHERE ... AND flg_terminado=0; ` with no SET XACT_ABORT ON, no transaction, no TRY/CATCH, no IF NOT EXISTS, no @@ROWCOUNT check, and no SET NOCOUNT ON`.
• Why it is wrong: the three statements are applied independently. If the INSERT succeeds and the first UPDATE fails (for example, as a deadlock victim against SWF_OUT_READ/SWF_OUT_ETL, which update the same master and work rows), the control row INI already exists while the master row is still AUT. The next tick (10 s later) selects the same folio again and inserts a second INI row; the MQ senders select cve_estatus = 'INI', so the same message can be published twice to the SWIFT gateway. Because the search procedure also reads with NOLOCK and nothing claims the row, two service instances (or an overlapping manual run) would double-insert as well. Whether a unique key on (folio, tipo_operacion) exists is unknown (the DDL is not in the repository); if one exists, the retry fails forever instead (see FR-6). Either way the unit of work is not all-or-nothing, and the C# caller cannot tell which statements ran.
• Recommended code: make it one transaction that claims the master row first and is a no-op on retry:
``sql SET NOCOUNT ON; SET XACT_ABORT ON; BEGIN TRAN; UPDATE dbo.tb_srv_swf_plan_trabajo_maestro SET cve_estatus='MQ', flg_con_error=0, fec_fin=GETDATE() WHERE tipo_operacion=@tipo_operacion AND folio=@icn AND cve_estatus='AUT'; IF @@ROWCOUNT = 1 BEGIN IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM dbo.tb_srv_swf_swift_out_ctl WHERE folio=@icn AND tipo_operacion=@tipo_operacion) INSERT INTO dbo.tb_srv_swf_swift_out_ctl (folio, tipo_operacion, cve_estatus, fec_adicion) VALUES (@icn, @tipo_operacion, 'INI', GETDATE()); UPDATE dbo.tb_srv_swf_plan_trabajo_detalle SET flg_terminado=1, flg_con_error=0, fec_fin=GETDATE(), fec_inicio=ISNULL(fec_inicio, GETDATE()) WHERE tipo_operacion=@tipo_operacion AND folio=@icn AND flg_terminado=0; END COMMIT; ` Add a unique key on the control table if the business rule is one INI` row per folio, and have the procedure return the affected-row count so the caller can log "ya procesado" instead of success.
• Benefit: removes the duplicate-send window on the highest-impact step of the outbound chain; makes retries safe.
• Suggested JIRA: PKDB-3 (High)
Rewrite Recommended
PKDB-FR-6 (Medium) - A failing folio is retried every tick forever with no alert, back-off, or retry cap
• File:line: OperacionesOUTPK.cs:42-56 (../../SWIFT/Outgoing/SWF_OUT_PK_DB/Infraestructura/Repositorio/OperacionesOUTPK.cs#L42)
• Current code: catch (Exception ex) { Config.EscribirLog($"ERROR|Interpreta_MensajesPKDB| {ex}", ...); }
• Why it is wrong: the per-item catch only logs. A permanently failing folio (constraint violation, bad data, locked row) stays in status AUT, so it is selected and fails again every tick, writing an ERROR with a full stack trace each time (about 8,600 per day at the fallback interval), and it never raises an alert, so operations learns about a stuck outbound SWIFT message only if someone reads the log. Other folios are still processed (good), but nothing records which folio is poisoned.
• Recommended code: count consecutive failures per folio in memory, raise one alert (Folio, TipoOperacion, exception) on the first failure and on reaching a threshold, back off or park the folio (flg_con_error = 1 in a dedicated procedure) after N failures, and log the folio in the error line.
• Benefit: a stuck message is visible within one tick and does not flood the log.
• Suggested JIRA: PKDB-6 (Medium)
PKDB-FR-9 (Medium) - The search query fetches large XML columns that are never used, reads with NOLOCK, and uses an implicit join, every tick
• File:line: DOperaciones.cs:8-12 (../../SWIFT/Outgoing/SWF_OUT_PK_DB/Aplicacion/DTOs/DOperaciones.cs#L8); SQL: 001 SP usp_BuscarOperacionesInsertarSWFOUTPKDB.sql:34-45 (../../StoredProcedures/MWD_DB/DBMDW/SWIFT/Outgoing/SWF_OUT_PK_DB/IT24/Procedures/001%20SP%20usp_BuscarOperacionesInsertarSWFOUTPKDB.sql#L34)
• Current code: the procedure returns cve_mensaje, nf_variables_xml and nf_mq_msg_etl for every pending row; C# reads only Folio and TipoOperacion (OperacionesOUTPK.cs:44,50 (../../SWIFT/Outgoing/SWF_OUT_PK_DB/Infraestructura/Repositorio/OperacionesOUTPK.cs#L44)). The join is the old comma form with WITH(NOLOCK) on both tables.
• Why it is wrong: the two XML payload columns (full SWIFT message variables and the MQ message body) are materialized into memory by the shared reader for every pending row and discarded, which grows with backlog size (for example, after the service has been down). NOLOCK on tables that READ/ETL update concurrently allows dirty reads, so a row can be selected while its authorizing transaction is still uncommitted or skipped/duplicated during page splits. The join to tb_srv_swf_trabajo is also only a filter here, so a master row without a work row is silently never processed.
• Recommended code: return only folio and tipo_operacion, use an explicit INNER JOIN/EXISTS, drop NOLOCK (or use READ_COMMITTED_SNAPSHOT), and cap the batch (TOP (@maxLote) ordered by fec_adicion); remove the three unused properties from DOperaciones.
• Benefit: lower memory/IO per tick, correct reads, bounded batches.
• Suggested JIRA: PKDB-9 (Medium)
