5. Architecture-Fit Note
Per the standing question in _ORCHESTRATION.md section 6 (_ORCHESTRATION.md), this is a scheduled/polling batch with a very small, stateless unit of work: select authorized rows, run one stored procedure per row. That shape is a poor fit for a long-lived Windows Service only in the sense that the polling interval, the window, and the lifecycle bookkeeping are more code than the work itself; it does not justify re-platforming. The sequence should be: (1) fix the defects that can silently stop or duplicate outbound SWIFT traffic (FR-1, FR-2, FR-3, FR-4); (2) add the missing tests and a real pipeline (FR-7, FR-8); (3) only then consider folding this step into SWF_OUT_ETL or the MQ sender as a single transactional hand-off, which would remove the intermediate polling hop entirely. That last option is a design decision that depends on unknowns this review cannot see: the required latency between authorization and send, who else writes tb_srv_swf_swift_out_ctl, whether the MQ senders are idempotent, the hosting constraints, and the SLA. The host inherits the base-class OnStart assignment described in GEN-14 (not reproduced under a real Service Control Manager).
